{"id":"assistant-f3873f7e-c62a-59a6-bec9-9b48b0879d01","sessionId":"6857faa7-23ce-b2aa-b505-6f6520514479","turnId":"turn-f660d1bd3142931a644d2d51b93ccbb8","inputEntryId":"user-turn-f660d1bd3142931a644d2d51b93ccbb8-1","createdAt":1794279975514,"updatedAt":1794279975514,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I understand the setup. The `kimi` lane uses `sandbox-exec -f sandbox/agent.sb` with a scratch `HOME` directory, and passes the real `HOME` via `-D HOME=<real home>`. The sandbox profile denies reading and writing to the real home directory.\n\nFor the `codex` lane, Codex CLI writes its final output to `-o \"$R/final.txt\"`, where `$R` is the run folder inside the user's home directory. When running under the sandbox, this write would be denied (as described in the colleague's note).\n\nThe fix is to:\n1. Create a scratch folder in `/tmp` for Codex's output\n2. Point Codex's `-o` flag to that scratch folder\n3. After `sandbox-exec` returns, copy the output file to the run folder\n\nLet me also check if there's a Codex installation path pattern I should follow:"}],"source":"harness","userDisplayContent":null,"outcome":{"type":"committed"}}
